# bench/labels/kubernetes-goat.truth - the HAND-AUTHORED ground truth for the
# kubernetes-goat `scenarios/` slice, pinned at bench/corpus.lock's
# `kubernetes-goat` commit.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE CORPUS.  kubernetes-goat's own ground truth is a prose
# walkthrough under `guide/`, one page per scenario, describing an ATTACK PATH
# rather than labelling the manifests - so it cannot be scored against
# directly, and the labels below were authored by reading the manifests.  They
# are committed, with a rationale beside every case, because a benchmark whose
# ground truth nobody can inspect is not a benchmark.
#
# NO TOOL OUTPUT OF ANY KIND WAS CONSULTED TO PRODUCE THEM (methodology rule
# R2).  The two labelling rules and the attribution rule are stated below so
# every call can be re-derived independently.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE UNIT IS THE KUBERNETES DOCUMENT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One case per `---`-separated document that declares a `kind:`, which is the
# same object a Kubernetes API server would create and the same object Checkov,
# KICS and Trivy each name in their own `resource` field.  Every document in
# the slice is labelled - none skipped - so a reader can check the set is
# complete rather than curated.  `line` is the document's real extent,
# computed from the file rather than transcribed.
#
# What the unit CANNOT express, and it bounds every number derived from here:
# a tool that flags a genuinely-misconfigured document for the WRONG REASON
# still scores a true positive.  Distinguishing "found the privileged
# container" from "found something else in the same Deployment" would need a
# defect vocabulary shared across scoursh, Checkov, KICS and Trivy; no such
# vocabulary exists, and inventing one here would put an unauditable dial
# between the corpus and the result.  The document-level unit has no dial.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS EXCLUDED, and why
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `scenarios/metadata-db/` is a Helm chart.  Its `templates/*.yaml` are Go
# templates whose `metadata.name` is `{{ include ... }}` rather than a name, so
# they are not Kubernetes manifests until `helm template` has rendered them.
# Scoring a plain-manifest scanner on them measures which tool guesses hardest
# at unrendered template syntax, which is not detection.  The exclusion is
# stated in bench/corpus.lock's own note as well, so it cannot be discovered
# only by reading this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULES, applied to every case below - identical to the ones
# bench/labels/terragoat-aws.truth states, deliberately, so the two IaC
# corpora are labelled to one standard rather than two.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R-REAL  `true` when a competent reviewer would REQUIRE this changed before
#         production.
# R-CLEAN `false` when the configuration as written is a defensible production
#         choice, EVEN IF a best-practice linter would comment on it.  A tool
#         flagging such a document is counted as a false positive, which is the
#         whole reason the clean cases are here.
# R-ATTR  A defect is attributed to the document whose OWN fields express it.
#         A document that is dangerous only because of what it references is
#         `false`, and the referenced document carries the label.
#
# Cases where the call is genuinely arguable are marked BORDERLINE in their own
# rationale and re-scored both ways in the leg README's sensitivity table.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT: bench/lib/truth.sh records - five 0x1f-separated fields plus a sixth.
#
#     <case><US><file><US><category><US><cwe><US><real><US><line-range>
#
# The sixth field is the document's line range and is what `bench/score.sh
# --match line` scores against.  `cwe` is EMPTY for every case here - see
# "what the unit cannot express" above - and the scorer renders the strict-CWE
# column for such a category as an explicit `no CWE in truth` cell rather than
# as a row of zeros.
#
# 35 cases: 19 genuinely misconfigured, 16 correctly configured.

# ==========================================================================
# batch-check/job.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Job/batch-check-job  (lines 1-17)
#   `image: madhuakula/k8s-goat-batch-check` carries no tag, so it resolves
#   to the mutable `latest` and what runs is whatever the registry points at
#   that minute. The pod spec sets no securityContext at all, so the
#   container runs as root with an unrestricted capability set and a
#   writable root filesystem, and no resource limits, so one job can starve
#   the node.
batch-check/job.yaml:Job/batch-check-jobbatch-check/job.yamlkubernetestrue1-17

# ==========================================================================
# build-code/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Deployment/build-code-deployment  (lines 1-24)
#   Untagged image, and no securityContext anywhere in the pod spec - root,
#   no dropped capabilities, allowPrivilegeEscalation left at its permissive
#   default. Resource limits are present but requests are not.
build-code/deployment.yaml:Deployment/build-code-deploymentbuild-code/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue1-24

# Service/build-code-service  (lines 25-36)
#   A ClusterIP service forwarding 3000 to 3000 inside the cluster. It
#   exposes nothing outside it and has no attribute that could be set
#   insecurely.
build-code/deployment.yaml:Service/build-code-servicebuild-code/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse25-36

# ==========================================================================
# cache-store/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Namespace/secure-middleware  (lines 1-5)
#   A bare namespace. It carries no pod-security-admission labels, which a
#   hardened cluster would add - but an unlabelled namespace is the
#   Kubernetes default and is a defensible production choice.
cache-store/deployment.yaml:Namespace/secure-middlewarecache-store/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse1-5

# Service/cache-store-service  (lines 6-18)
#   ClusterIP on 6379. In-cluster only.
cache-store/deployment.yaml:Service/cache-store-servicecache-store/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse6-18

# Deployment/cache-store-deployment  (lines 19-39)
#   A Redis-shaped cache with an untagged image, no securityContext and no
#   resource limits. Root by default, on the pod that holds the cache.
cache-store/deployment.yaml:Deployment/cache-store-deploymentcache-store/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue19-39

# ==========================================================================
# docker-bench-security/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# DaemonSet/docker-bench-security  (lines 1-138)
#   hostPID, hostIPC and hostNetwork all true, runAsUser 0, `privileged:
#   true` on both the init container and the main container,
#   CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL added, and the host root filesystem plus the container
#   runtime socket mounted in. A privileged container with the docker socket
#   is a full node takeover by design; this is the least arguable case in
#   the corpus.
docker-bench-security/deployment.yaml:DaemonSet/docker-bench-securitydocker-bench-security/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue1-138

# ==========================================================================
# health-check/deployment-simple-alternative.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Deployment/health-check-deployment-simple  (lines 1-63)
#   `privileged: true` and three hostPath mounts of the container runtime
#   socket. Write access to the runtime socket is equivalent to root on the
#   node, `readOnly: true` on the mount notwithstanding - the volume itself
#   is a hostPath with no read-only enforcement at the volume level.
health-check/deployment-simple-alternative.yaml:Deployment/health-check-deployment-simplehealth-check/deployment-simple-alternative.yamlkubernetestrue1-63

# Service/health-check-service  (lines 64-74)
#   ClusterIP on 80. In-cluster only.
health-check/deployment-simple-alternative.yaml:Service/health-check-servicehealth-check/deployment-simple-alternative.yamlkubernetesfalse64-74

# ==========================================================================
# health-check/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Deployment/health-check-deployment  (lines 1-99)
#   The init container is `privileged: true` and mounts the host root
#   filesystem at /host-root; the application container is `privileged:
#   true` and receives the container runtime socket the init container
#   linked. Two separate privileged containers in one pod spec.
health-check/deployment.yaml:Deployment/health-check-deploymenthealth-check/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue1-99

# Service/health-check-service  (lines 100-110)
#   ClusterIP on 80. In-cluster only.
health-check/deployment.yaml:Service/health-check-servicehealth-check/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse100-110

# ==========================================================================
# hidden-in-layers/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Job/hidden-in-layers  (lines 1-17)
#   Untagged image and no securityContext - root, unrestricted capabilities,
#   writable root filesystem - and no resource limits.
hidden-in-layers/deployment.yaml:Job/hidden-in-layershidden-in-layers/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue1-17

# ==========================================================================
# hunger-check/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Namespace/big-monolith  (lines 2-6)
#   A bare namespace. Same reasoning as Namespace/secure-middleware.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:Namespace/big-monolithhunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse2-6

# Role/secret-reader  (lines 7-16)
#   `resources: ["*"]` with get, watch and list on the core API group. The
#   wildcard includes Secrets, so this role reads every credential in the
#   namespace - which is exactly what the two Secrets below it hold.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:Role/secret-readerhunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue7-16

# RoleBinding/secret-reader-binding  (lines 17-30)
#   ATTRIBUTION. It binds the role above to the service account below; the
#   wildcard that makes the grant dangerous is written in the Role and is
#   labelled there. Crediting a tool for both would double-count one
#   mistake.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:RoleBinding/secret-reader-bindinghunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse17-30

# ServiceAccount/big-monolith-sa  (lines 31-36)
#   A service account. A service account holds no permissions of its own -
#   what it can do is decided by the bindings that name it.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:ServiceAccount/big-monolith-sahunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse31-36

# Secret/vaultapikey  (lines 37-45)
#   An API key committed to the repository. `data:` is base64, which is an
#   encoding and not encryption - the value decodes with one command, and it
#   is in version control forever.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:Secret/vaultapikeyhunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue37-45

# Secret/webhookapikey  (lines 46-54)
#   A second committed API key, same shape as vaultapikey.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:Secret/webhookapikeyhunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue46-54

# Deployment/hunger-check-deployment  (lines 55-82)
#   Untagged image, no securityContext, resource limits commented out, and
#   it runs under big-monolith-sa - the service account the Role above lets
#   read every Secret in the namespace.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:Deployment/hunger-check-deploymenthunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue55-82

# Service/hunger-check-service  (lines 83-94)
#   ClusterIP on 8080. In-cluster only.
hunger-check/deployment.yaml:Service/hunger-check-servicehunger-check/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse83-94

# ==========================================================================
# insecure-rbac/setup.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# ServiceAccount/superadmin  (lines 1-6)
#   A service account in kube-system. It holds nothing until something binds
#   a role to it, which the next document does and is labelled for.
insecure-rbac/setup.yaml:ServiceAccount/superadmininsecure-rbac/setup.yamlkubernetesfalse1-6

# ClusterRoleBinding/superadmin  (lines 7-18)
#   Binds the built-in `cluster-admin` ClusterRole to a service account.
#   Anything that obtains that token owns the cluster. Attributed here
#   rather than to the ServiceAccount because this document is where the
#   grant is written - `cluster-admin` itself is a built-in and is not
#   declared in this corpus.
insecure-rbac/setup.yaml:ClusterRoleBinding/superadmininsecure-rbac/setup.yamlkubernetestrue7-18

# ==========================================================================
# internal-proxy/deployment.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Deployment/internal-proxy-deployment  (lines 1-40)
#   Two containers, both with untagged images and neither with a
#   securityContext. Resource limits and requests ARE set on both, which is
#   the one thing this workload gets right.
internal-proxy/deployment.yaml:Deployment/internal-proxy-deploymentinternal-proxy/deployment.yamlkubernetestrue1-40

# Service/internal-proxy-api-service  (lines 41-53)
#   ClusterIP on 3000. In-cluster only.
internal-proxy/deployment.yaml:Service/internal-proxy-api-serviceinternal-proxy/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse41-53

# Service/internal-proxy-info-app-service  (lines 54-67)
#   BORDERLINE, resolved against flagging. A NodePort on 30003. NodePort
#   exposes the service on every node's address, which is a real increase in
#   reachable surface - but it is also the standard way to expose a service
#   on a cluster with no load-balancer integration, and whether it is safe
#   is decided by the node firewall rather than by this document. A
#   defensible production choice under R-CLEAN. See the leg README's
#   sensitivity table.
internal-proxy/deployment.yaml:Service/internal-proxy-info-app-serviceinternal-proxy/deployment.yamlkubernetesfalse54-67

# ==========================================================================
# kube-bench-security/master-job.yaml
# ==========================================================================

# Job/kube-bench-master  (lines 2-113)
#   BORDERLINE, resolved toward flagging. `image: aquasec/kube-bench:latest`
#   is a mutable tag on a workload that reads the control plane's own
#   configuration, and `hostPID: true` is broader than the read-only file
#   mounts it actually uses. Running a CIS benchmark as a Job is legitimate;
#   running it from an unpinned image with the host process namespace is
#   not. See the leg README's sensitivity table.
kube-bench-security/master-job.yaml:Job/kube-bench-masterkube-bench-security/master-job.yamlkubernetestrue2-113

